# Sound critics: shared rules (read with SHARED.md)
You CANNOT hear the film. Your ears are (a) MEASURED AUDIO DATA from scripts/measure_audio.py, which you trust over guesses, and (b) the AUDIO TIMELINE (every VO line, music state and SFX cue, with times) plus the frames, which show what is on screen when.
Film sound has layers: DIALOGUE/VO · FOLEY (human-scale actions) · HARD SFX (impacts, whooshes, risers, UI clicks) · AMBIENCE/ROOM TONE (the world's air) · MUSIC (score or needle drop) · SILENCE (designed, never digital dead air).
Concepts: diegetic (in-world) vs non-diegetic (score, narrator); sound perspective (level, EQ and reverb match the distance from camera); sound bridges (J-cut: the next scene's sound starts early; L-cut: the previous one carries over); leitmotif (a recurring sound idea tied to a character or product).
Reference benchmarks: SMITH drops the music to silence before the tagline; Codex holds the score until the "ignition" at ~40%; a16z runs one VO spine with L-cut soundbites; the score's hits land on cuts.
